---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { implementationDoc, specificationDoc } from '../../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="MCP over sempods — agents reach independent data spaces"
  description="Per-pod MCP and hosted multi-pod MCP give agents access to linked knowledge under each pod's authorization decisions."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">concepts · MCP</p>
  <h1>Let agents come to the knowledge</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    MCP gives an agent tools for finding and reading pod data. The useful
    part is where the knowledge stays: in independent pods whose owners
    decide what the connection may reach.
  </p>
  <h2>One pod, one optional surface</h2>
  <p>
    A pod implementing the MCP module exposes <code>/_system/mcp</code>.
    Its tools can list Contexts, call <code>find</code>, fetch a resource,
    inspect properties and run sandboxed SPARQL. An agent combines those
    operations into a retrieval loop.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tools add no authority. Authentication and server-resolved grants
    determine their reach, just as they do for HTTP resource operations.
  </p>
  <h2>Several pods, one agent connection</h2>
  <p>
    The Kotlin hosted MCP service maintains connections to multiple pods.
    An agent can gather results from independent data spaces, with each pod
    enforcing its own permissions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hosted service mediates those requests and handles connection
    credentials. Choose its operator as part of your trust decision. The
    source data remains in the pods; the agent can combine the returned
    facts for its task.
  </p>
  <div class="proof">
    <h3>An answer can cross a boundary</h3>
    <p>
      An agent could read public events from a venue and private plans from
      your pod. The venue decides what is public. You decide what the agent
      may read from your plans. Combining an answer transfers no authority
      from one pod to the other.
    </p>
  </div>
  <h2>Check the implementation you connect to</h2>
  <p>
    MCP is optional in the specification. Kotlin 0.2 supplies both per-pod and
    hosted surfaces, with setup described in their guides. A running host's
    configuration and revision need their own verification.
  </p>
  <p>
    The contract requires conformance discovery. Kotlin 0.2 does not yet
    serve <code>/_system/conformance</code>; its release notes record the
    deviation. Use its documented capabilities rather than assuming that
    discovery is available.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href={specificationDoc('spec/modules/mcp.md')}>MCP module</a>{' '}
    <a href={implementationDoc('docs/mcp/README.md')}>Per-pod MCP guide</a>{' '}
    <a href={implementationDoc('sempods-mcp/docs/README.md')}>Hosted MCP guide</a>{' '}
    <a href="/concepts/graph-retrieval">Follow the retrieval loop</a>
  </p>
</Base>
